%S A397140 0,0,1,0,2,1,4,3,4,5,3,3,6,5,10,11,14,5,4,7,8,8,20,18,12,15,13,26,12,
%T A397140 20,10,17,30,12,35,11,16,19,40,41,44,15,31,20,37,15,10,22,56,22,54,38,
%U A397140 16,40,64,64,65,19,28,38,32,71,30,42,28,77,24,22,86,37,74,88,39,39,34,94
%N A397140 a(n) = number of positive g < prime(n) such that g and g+1 are both primitive roots modulo prime(n).
%C A397140 a(n) = 0 only for prime(n) = 2, 3 and 7. Cohen proved that every finite field of order other than 2, 3 or 7 contains a pair of consecutive primitive roots.
%C A397140 a(n) is not determined by A008330(n) = phi(prime(n)-1): the deviation from the asymptotic main term is of order sqrt(prime(n)).
%D A397140 S. D. Cohen, Pairs of primitive roots, Mathematika 32 (1985), 276-285.
%H A397140 Daniel Okwor, <a href="/A397140/b397140.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..9592</a>
%H A397140 S. D. Cohen, T. Oliveira e Silva and T. Trudgian, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.6210">On consecutive primitive elements in a finite field</a>, arXiv:1410.6210 [math.NT], 2014; Bull. London Math. Soc. 47 (2015), 418-426.
%F A397140 a(n) ~ phi(p-1)^2/(p-1), where p = prime(n), the count predicted if being a primitive root were independent at g and g+1.
%e A397140 prime(5) = 11 has primitive roots 2, 6, 7 and 8. The consecutive pairs among them are (6,7) and (7,8), so a(5) = 2.
%o A397140 (PARI) a(n) = my(p=prime(n), c=0); for(x=1, p-2, if(znorder(Mod(x,p))==p-1 && znorder(Mod(x+1,p))==p-1, c++)); c
%Y A397140 Cf. A229899, A261388, A261438, A268397, A060749, A008330, A249412, A229910.
%K A397140 nonn
%O A397140 1,5
